# The counted-day extracts are the tree's durable copy of a measurement that
# otherwise disappears. architecture/vllm.md §10's figures come from engine status
# lines that are byte-rotated (~17 h per 10 MB file at this load), so a counted day
# is unverifiable within a week unless its extract is committed — and the working
# copies have been landing in /tmp, which is tmpfs, where a reboot is what deletes
# them (#1812).
#
# The root .gitignore ends every new JSON in the tree (`*.json`), which is right for
# runtime data and wrong here: this is the directory whose whole job is to hold a
# fixture per counted day. The two extracts that predate #1812 got in only because
# someone force-added them past the rule, which is invisible to the next round and
# had already left the 09-28 reading uncommitted. The negation below is the visible
# version of that, scoped to the extract family rather than to all JSON, so a
# stray runtime JSON in this directory is still ignored — and so counting the NEXT
# day, which §10 owes, is one `git add` rather than a rule nobody can see.
#
# The root file itself is off-limits to the self-modification loop (denied in
# scripts/automod/spec.py), so a file like this one is the only route that does not
# end in a human typing `git add -f` for every counted day.
#
# The second family below is not a status-line extract: it is the frozen per-day
# fleet totals (turns / misses / re-prefill tokens / chat turns / session-kind mix)
# that §10's trigger-(ii) routine-exceedance claim rests on, read out of `usage.db`
# before the row-level churn the same database keeps makes the same claim
# un-re-derivable (#1919). It gets its own negation rather than a vllm_prefix_miss_*
# name because those bytes never came from a status line, and a fixture named for the
# wrong source is worse than one that is ignored.
!vllm_prefix_miss_*.json
!usage_day_totals_*.json

# The third family is not a counted day either: it is the `summary.overall.label_agreement`
# block of one nightly eval baseline, which #1938's tests re-derive their quoted figures
# from. Two things make the durable copy necessary rather than convenient. The full
# baseline is retention-swept (`eval/baselines` keeps 40 nightly runs), so the run this
# item's numbers came from is on a path that is scheduled to disappear; and the vault
# witness of the same block (`backlog/data/nightly-20260930-20260930-060339.json`) cannot
# be read from a test, because the gate runs with HOME at the round home where
# `~/obsidian` does not exist — a node that opened it would skip, and a skipping node
# pins nothing. Scoped to the label_agreement extract rather than to nightly baselines
# whole, so a real baseline JSON is still ignored: it carries gold quotes and query text.
!nightly-*-label_agreement.json

# The fourth family is a report this job wrote about itself. #2119's premise is one
# `stray_retention` block — a 1,250,205,696 B retired backup held from reclaim by a
# `-wal` 2.0 ms newer than its own main file, `planned` empty, `dry_run` false — and the
# item quotes numbers out of it. Its durable home is the vault witness under
# `backlog/data/` (clause 6), which a test cannot open for exactly the reason the
# paragraph above gives, and this family holds the same bytes again for that reason
# rather than as an extract: clause 6's own re-derive is `wc -l` of the whole report,
# and a block cut out of it cannot answer a line count. Byte-identical to the vault copy,
# md5 16647dfffb2d18a1a3ef84bd4059f91e, and
# `test_the_held_backup_witness_bytes_still_carry_the_measured_hold` re-derives every
# quoted figure from them. Scoped to this job's report family rather than to all JSON: a
# live report of this name belongs to `~/lloyd-data/_pipeline/reflection/`, never here,
# so the negation only ever reaches a witness somebody chose to commit.
!qmd-index-maintenance-*.json

# The fifth family is a session transcript's WITNESS — an extract, not the transcript.
# #2176's premise is that no instrument can say whether a shipped prompt-fix was in the
# prompt a live process assembled, and one of the three candidates for "there already is
# one" was the transcript itself. It is not: in
# `~/lloyd-data/sessions/20261004_023513_autotriage_33f5.json` (917 lines, 113,563 B, 39
# messages, no `system` role anywhere) `messages[0]` is the USER turn, 16,926 chars, and
# does not carry the paragraph `_data_home_hint` returns. `sessions/` is retention-swept
# the way the two families above are, and the vault copy under `backlog/data/` (clause 6)
# is unreadable from a node for the reason the paragraph above gives, so the extract lives
# here too. An extract rather than the whole file because the whole file is a triage
# session's prompt and reply text — the thing the manifest's retention policy exists not to
# retain — while every figure #2176 quotes is about messages[0] and is carried here with
# the source's digests beside it. `test_the_committed_witness_bytes_still_carry_the_measured_gap`
# re-derives them. Scoped to this name family so a transcript someone drops here by
# accident stays ignored.
!session_witness_*.json
